g is nice and white, it\'s red.\n\nBut let\'s see if Harry is red.From point 6: If Harry is red, then Harry is round.But we already know from point 8 that if Harry is rough, he\'s round.So, whether Harry is red or not, he\'s round.\n\nWait, but point 10 says that round, red things are white.So, if Harry is square and blue, then he\'s black. But from point 11, if Harry is mean and black, then he\'s blue. So, if Harry is mean and black, he\'s blue. But we do know yet if Harry is black.\n\nWait, from point 5: All smooth things are black. So, if Harry is smooth, he\'s black. But from point 4, Harry is mean and soft. We do know yet if Harry is smooth. Let\'s check point 12: If something is square, then it\'s smooth. Since Harry is square (from point 8), he must be smooth. Therefore, Harry is smooth, which means from point 5, Harry is black.\n\nNow, if Harry is black, then from point 11: All mean, black things are blue. So, since Harry is mean and black, he must be blue.\n\nSo, Harry is blue, black, mean, soft, and square.\n\nNow, from point 9: Mean, blue things are small. Since Harry is mean and blue, he must be small.\n\nTherefore, Harry is small.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Harry is mean and soft, so he\'s square (point 8). Being square means he\'s smooth (point 12). Being smooth means he\'s black (point 5). Being black and mean means he\'s blue (point 11). Being blue and mean means he\'s small (point 9). So no, Harry is small. Therefore, the option is ']]
