res the tiger.From fact 7, the squirrel ignores the bear.Fact 14 says that if something ignores the bear, it doesn\'t need the tiger.So, the squirrel doesn\'t need the tiger.Now, for the tiger to avoid the squirrel, fact 19 says the tiger must be cruel and avoid the mouse..\n\nIs the tiger cruel?The bear is cruel (2), and the bear is avoiding the tiger.Maybe being avoided by a cruel bear makes the tiger cruel.So, the tiger is cruel.Now, does the tiger avoid the mouse?The mouse avoids the tiger (6), but that doesn\'t mean the tiger avoids the mouse.Unless there\'s a rule that if something is avoided by another, it must avoid back, but I don\'t think that\'s stated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger doesn\'t need the mouse because the mouse is avoiding the tiger.Wait, fact 18 says if the mouse is red and avoids something, that something doesn\'t need the tiger.But the mouse is red because the bear is red, and the mouse avoids the bear.So, the mouse is red.Therefore, if the mouse avoids the tiger, the tiger doesn\'t need the mouse.But the tiger is avoiding the mouse?Wait, no, the mouse is avoiding the tiger.So, the mouse is avoiding the tiger, which is red.So, according to fact 18, the tiger doesn\'t need the mouse.But does that mean the tiger avoids the mouse?Not necessarily..\n\nWait, fact 18 says if the mouse is red and avoids something, then that something doesn\'t need the tiger.So, the mouse is red and avoids the tiger, so the tiger doesn\'t need the mouse.But does that mean the tiger avoids the mouse?Not necessarily.Unless there\'s a rule that if something doesn\'t need another, it must avoid it, but I don\'t think that\'s stated.\n\nSo, the tiger doesn\'t need the mouse because the mouse is red and avoids it.But does that mean the tiger avoids the mouse?Maybe not.So, the tiger might not avoid the mouse, which means the condition for the tiger to avoid the squirrel (fact 19) is met because the tiger has avoided the mouse yet.\n\nWait, but the mouse is avoiding the tiger (6), so maybe the tiger is being avoided by the mouse, which makes the tiger not need the mouse.But does that make the tiger avoid the mouse?I\'m not sure.Maybe the tiger does avoid the mouse because the mouse is avoiding the tiger, but that\'s not necessarily a reciprocal relationship.\n\nSo, if the tiger does avoid the mouse, then the tiger is cruel and avoiding the mouse, so fact 19 does apply, and the tiger does avoid the squirrel.Therefore, the squirrel does ignore the tiger because the tiger is avoiding the squirrel.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that the mouse is avoiding the tiger means the tiger is being avoided by the mouse, which makes the tiger not need the mouse, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean the tiger avoids the mouse.So, the tiger might not avoid the mouse, meaning it does meet the condition to avoid the squirrel.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel does ignore the tiger because the tiger is avoiding the squirrel\n. Therefore, the option is ']]
