h means the squirrel doesn\'t visit the mouse (point 17). But that doesn\'t mean the mouse is not visiting the squirrel.\n\nSo, the mouse isn\'t visiting the bear and the mouse isn\'t visiting the rabbit, but the mouse is necessarily not visiting the squirrel. Therefore, the bear is being visited by the squirrel because the mouse is visiting the squirrel.\n\nWait, but point 18 says if the mouse doesn\'t visit the bear and the mouse doesn\'t visit the squirrel, then the squirrel doesn\'t visit the bear. So, for the squirrel not to visit the bear, the mouse must not be visiting both the bear and the squirrel.\n\nBut from point 17, the mouse isn\'t visiting the rabbit, which makes it not need the squirrel, so the squirrel doesn\'t visit the mouse. But that doesn\'t mean the mouse is not visiting the squirrel. So, the mouse isn\'t visiting the bear, but is the squirrel. Therefore, the squirrel visits the bear.\n\nSo, the bear is being visited by the squirrel. Therefore, the bear needs the squirrel because the squirrel is visiting it.\n\nWait, but the squirrel isn\'t visiting the mouse because it doesn\'t need the squirrel (point 17). So, the squirrel is not visiting the mouse, but the bear. Therefore, the bear needs the squirrel because the squirrel is visiting it.\n\nSo, the statement "The bear needs the squirrel" would be false because the squirrel is visiting the bear.\n\nWait, but I\'m completely sure. Let me try to summarize:.\n\n- Mouse is warm (avoiding bear) → doesn\'t need squirrel (point 13).\n- Squirrel doesn\'t need to be visited by mouse (point 17).\n- Mouse isn\'t visiting the rabbit (point 4).\n- Mouse isn\'t visiting the bear (point 2).\n- Squirrel isn\'t visiting the mouse (point 17).\n- Squirrel is visiting the bear because the mouse is visiting the squirrel.\n- Therefore, the bear needs the squirrel because the squirrel is visiting it.\n. Therefore, the option is ']] 75%|#######5  | 450/600 [51:14<19:16,  7.71s/it]
