nd old, then they are smooth. So Bob, being mean and old, wouldn\'t be smooth.\n\nBut wait, statement 12 says if someone is warm and loud, they are big. Since Bob is small, he can be warm and loud. But Bob is warm, so he can be loud. So Bob is warm and loud.\n\nBut from statement 16, if Bob is mean and old, he\'s smooth. So Bob isn\'t smooth. But the question is asking if Bob is smooth. So if Bob isn\'t smooth, then the statement "Bob is smooth" would be true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Bob is small (statement 3). Small people are old (statement 17), so Bob is old. Old people are warm (statement 18), so Bob is warm. From statement 14, warm and small people are flat, so Bob is flat. From statement 15, if Bob is flat, he\'s mean. So Bob is mean. From statement 16, if Bob is mean and old, he\'s smooth. Therefore, Bob isn\'t smooth.\n\nSo the statement "Bob is smooth" would be true because Bob isn\'t smooth.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s no contradiction. If Bob isn\'t smooth, is that impossible? Let me see.\n\nFrom statement 16: If someone is mean and old, then they aren\'t smooth. So Bob, being mean and old, isn\'t smooth. So Bob isn\'t smooth.\n\nTherefore, the statement "Bob is smooth" is true.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any other angle. Maybe Bob is nice? But from statement 15, if Bob is flat, he\'s mean. And from statement 14, Bob is flat because he\'s warm and small. So Bob can\'t be mean. Therefore, he can\'t be smooth.. Therefore, the option is ']]
