e, which seems contradictory.Wait, no, fact 16 says all white things are green, so if Harry is white, he\'s green.But fact 12 says all green things are blue, so Harry is blue.But fact 7 says he\'s white.That seems like a contradiction.Hmm, maybe I\'m missing something.\n\nWait, let me check again.Fact 16: All white things are green.So if Harry is white, he must be green.But fact 12: All green things are blue.So Harry, being green, must be blue.But fact 7 says Harry is white.That\'s a problem because if he\'s white, he\'s green, and if he\'s green, he\'s blue.So Harry can\'t be both white and green and blue at the same time.That seems impossible.Maybe I made a mistake.\n\nWait, no, fact 7 says Harry is white, so from fact 16, he\'s green.But fact 12 says all green things are blue, so he\'s blue.So Harry is both white and blue?That\'s possible because fact 16 and 12 don\'t contradict each other; they just establish that white things are green, and green things are blue.So being white implies not green, which implies not blue. So Harry is not white, not green, and not blue.\n\nNow, moving on. Fact 14: If Harry is white, then he\'s old. Since Harry is not white, from fact 14, he must be old. So that would make Harry old. But let me check if there\'s any other information that might affect this.\n\nLooking at Erin: She\'s old (fact 5). From fact 13, if something is old, it\'s not white. So Erin is not white. But from fact 11, if Erin is not green, she\'s not white. But Erin is old, so she\'s not white. But fact 16 says all not white things are not green, so Erin is not green. But fact 12 says all not green things are not blue, so Erin is not blue. Then, from fact 15: If Erin is not nice and not blue, she\'s old. We know Erin is old, so that fits because she\'s not nice and not blue. So that\'s consistent.\n\nWait, but the question is about Harry. So from fact 14, since Harry is not white, he\'s old. So that would make Harry old. But let me check if there\'s any other condition that might make him young.\n\nLooking at fact 13: If something is old, it\'s not white. So being white implies being old. So if Harry is not white, he\'s old. So that seems to confirm it.\n\nBut wait, let me check if there\'s any other way Harry could be young. For example, if there\'s a rule that says if something is not quiet, then being not blue makes it not nice, but I don\'t think that affects Harry\'s age directly.\n\nSo, putting it all together: Harry is not white (fact 7), which makes him not green (fact 16), which makes him not blue (fact 12). Then, from fact 14, being not white means he\'s old. So Harry is old.\n\nWait, but I\'m a bit confused because earlier I thought there was a contradiction between Harry being not white and not blue, but actually, fact 16 and 12 don\'t contradict because being white implies not green, which implies not blue. So Harry is not white, not green, and not blue, and from fact 14, he\'s old.. Therefore, the option is ']] 88%|########7 | 527/600 [59:02<06:29,  5.34s/it]
