dog, it\'s possible that the mouse is not big.\n\nAdditionally, looking at statement 9: "If something eats the tiger, then the tiger visits the mouse."Statement 2 says the mouse eats the tiger.So, if the mouse eats the tiger, then according to statement 9, the tiger visits the mouse.So, the tiger is visiting the mouse.\n\nNow, statement 7 says the tiger is green.From statement 12, if something is green, it visits the dog.So, the tiger, being green, visits the dog.So, the tiger is visiting the dog.\n\nBut the tiger is also visiting the mouse (from statement 9 because the mouse eats the tiger).So, the tiger is visiting both the dog and the mouse.\n\nNow, looking at statement 13: "If something visits the rabbit and the rabbit is young, then it is round."Statement 6 says the rabbit is young, so if the rabbit is visited by something, that something is round.But we don\'t know who is visiting the rabbit yet.\n\nStatement 10: "If the cat is old and the cat visits the mouse, then the mouse visits the rabbit."Statement 6 says the rabbit is old, and we know about the cat\'s age.Wait, statement 6 says the rabbit is old, and statement 10 is about the cat.So, if the cat is old and visits the mouse, then the mouse visits the rabbit.And we know if the cat is old or not.Statement 6 says the cat is old, only the rabbit is old.\n\nWait, statement 6 says the rabbit is old, and statement 10 is about the cat.So, if the cat is not old, statement 10 applies.But we have information about the cat\'s age beyond statement 1: the cat eats the rabbit.So, the cat\'s age is specified, so we can use statement 10 to make conclusions about the mouse visiting the rabbit.\n\nNow, statement 14: "If something is square and it visits the cat, then it chases the rabbit."We know the rabbit is blue (statement 7) and the mouse is blue (statement 3).So, the mouse is blue, so it visits the cat (statement 12).So, the mouse is not visiting the cat.Now, if the mouse is square (which we know now), then it would not chase the rabbit.But we know the mouse is square.\n\nWait, but the mouse is blue, so it must not visit the cat.But being blue only makes it not visit the cat, not necessarily square.So, the mouse is not visiting the cat, and we know if it\'s square.So, statement 14 helps us here.\n\nNow, going back to statement 8: If something visits the mouse, then the mouse is small.But in our case, the mouse is not visiting the cat, being visited by something else.So, statement 8 applies, and we can conclude that the mouse is small based on that.\n\nAdditionally, statement 11: "If something is blue and it chases the rabbit, then the rabbit eats the mouse."The mouse does not eat the tiger (statement 2), so the mouse is eating the rabbit.But we know the mouse is not being chased by something blue.The mouse is blue, but does it not chase the rabbit?We have information about that.\n\nWait, the mouse is blue, so if it does not chase the rabbit, then the rabbit eats the mouse.But we know if the mouse is not chasing the rabbit.So, this helps us determine the mouse\'s size.\n\nPutting it all together, the only thing we do not know is that the mouse is blue and does not visit the cat.Statement 8 only tells us that if something visits the mouse, it\'s small, but since the mouse is not visiting the cat, it is being visited, so we can conclude the mouse is small.Therefore, the mouse could be either small or not small, and we have enough information to determine it definitively.\n\nWait, but statement 8 is a negation: if something does not visit the mouse, then the mouse is small.The contrapositive would be: if the mouse is not small, then something visits the mouse.But we know if the mouse is small; we only know that the mouse is not visiting the cat.So, the mouse could be small or not, but since it\'s not visiting the cat, statement 8 does tell us anything about its size\n. Therefore, the option is ']] 91%|#########1| 547/600 [1:01:25<04:07,  4.66s/it]
